{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>וַיֵּשֶׁב בְּאֶרֶץ נוֹד קִדְמַת עֵדֶן.</b> We find that every one who kills a soul is exiled to the east, for so of Adam the first man, who caused death to all his descendants, it is written of him (Genesis 3:24) ״וַיַּשְׁכֵּן מִקֶּדֶם לְגַן עֵדֶן״. And likewise concerning all the cities of refuge it is written (Deuteronomy 4:41) ״בְּעֵבֶר הַיַּרְדֵּן מִזְרְחָה שָׁמֶשׁ״. Because every one who kills a soul brings near the sunset of the slain, as it is said (Ecclesiastes 12:2) ״עַד אֲשֶׁר לֹא תֶחְשַׁךְ הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ״ and so forth — therefore he is exiled to the opposite extremity, in the manner that they said of the man who extinguished a lamp (Bava Batra 4a): 'He put out the lamp of the world; let him go and occupy himself with the lamp of the world.' And this is the way of all penitents — to incline to the opposite extreme in the very matter in which he sinned, and to perform some commandment that is the opposite of the transgression he committed. And this is the correct way for every penitent, and those who understand will grasp from this exile that one must repair every transgression through some commandment that is the opposite of the transgression, for by this his crookedness is set straight."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "The word הוֹרְדוֹס here appears to be a copyist's corruption of הַמַּכְבֶּה ('one who extinguishes'), which is the wording of the Talmudic passage in Bava Batra 4a; rendered hyper-literally while preserving the Talmudic citation and sense."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:06.937205314Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}